if(Q.apoyo_popular<0){Q.apoyo_popular=0;} if(Q.pol_intl<0){Q.pol_intl=0;}
!}

= El invierno de El Quemado

[? if ch_p2 = "incorporar" : They come into the zone and the column's food problem, which was already the column's worst problem, becomes arithmetic that does not work. You do it anyway. Fifty people are fed out of caches meant for forty fighters, three of the older boys end up carrying rifles within the month because there is nothing else for them to do, and the whole band eats less all winter without anybody being asked to vote on it. What you get in exchange cannot be spent: those fifty people are now organically part of this thing, not supporters of it, and they will lie to a soldier with a rifle in their mouth and mean it. ?][? if ch_p2 = "reubicar" : You walk them three valleys over to people who take them in without being asked twice, which is the sierra doing the thing the sierra does. They live. The children are in a school by the spring. And the valley you moved them out of is now an empty valley — no eyes, no maize, no houses to sleep in on a bad night — and within two months there is a permanent army post in the ruins, because the army also understands what an emptied valley is for. You saved fifty people and lost a district, and both of those are the same decision. ?][? if ch_p2 = "reconstruir" : You put the entire column on roof beams for five weeks. No operations, no ambushes, nothing that would look like a war to anyone watching from a plane — just forty armed men and women cutting timber, hauling adobe, digging out a granary, and being fed by people who have nothing and insist. It is the single least military month of the campaign and it does more than the last four ambushes combined. The village stands again because you built it. Every hamlet within thirty kilometres now knows the specific difference between the army, which burns, and the guerrilla, which came back and rebuilt. The army knows exactly where you were for five weeks, too. ?][? if ch_p2 = "testigo" : You give them the one thing you actually have in surplus, which is contact with the outside: a priest with a tape recorder, a route to a bishop who will not be intimidated, a set of names and dates that ends up in a mimeographed bulletin in Mexico City and then, six months later, in a paragraph of a report in Geneva. It buys them nothing this winter. No roofs, no maize. But El Quemado becomes a word that exists outside Guerrero, and a state that has spent four years insisting nothing is happening in the sierra has to spend the next year explaining a village that officially does not exist.[? if brutalidad >= 4 : The state's reply writes itself, and the reply is a list of your own ajusticiamientos, and it lands. Half the audience decides this is two sets of armed men and no innocents. ?] ?]

- @root: Continuar.

# =====================================================================
# EL SECUESTRO DEL SENADOR — the act that made Lucio and doomed him. Now in three beats.
# =====================================================================
@ev_figueroa
title: El secuestro del senador
new-page: true
face-image: img/congreso.jpg
subtitle: 1974 — the loudest thing a foco can do
tags: event
view-if: banda = "sierra" and f_phase = "marcha" and f_hombres >= 20 and apoyo_popular >= 45 and year >= 1974 and f_figueroa = 0
priority: 5
max-visits: 1
on-arrival: {! Q.f_figueroa=1; Q.ch_p1=""; Q.ch_p2=""; Q.ch_res=""; !}

= El secuestro del senador

The chance arrives the way it arrived for Lucio: as a piece of gossip from a chauffeur.

A senator — a rich man, a cacique of caciques, a candidate for the governorship — is moving through the sierra with one car of guard and the confidence of a man who has never once been told no in this state. For four days he is *takeable* by any twenty people with rifles and the nerve.

Everyone in the camp already knows. You can see them not asking. Because a kidnapping like this is the loudest thing a foco can do — ransom enough to arm the column for a year, comrades out of Lecumberri, the spectacle of the state's own aristocracy sitting on a stool in a hut being fed beans by the people it despises. And because everybody has also read how this story goes: it is precisely the act that turned the entire weight of the Mexican army onto the sierra de Atoyac and ended Lucio, within months, in a coffee grove called El Otatal.

- @fig_tomar: Take him. *(everything, and the storm)*
- @fig_dejar: Let him ride out. A foco that survives is worth more than one that is famous. *(nothing changes, which is the point)*

@fig_dejar
new-page: true
on-arrival: {! Q.ch_p1="dejar"; Q.f_moral+=2; Q.disciplina=(Q.disciplina||65)+3; !}

= Se fue por la carretera

You let him ride out of the sierra with his confidence intact and his story untold, and you do not explain the reasoning to the column because the reasoning is *I have read how this ends* and that is not a thing a commander can say out loud.

Half the band thinks you are a coward and the other half thinks you are a strategist, and the annoying truth is that nothing whatsoever will happen to prove either of them right. This is the part of the war nobody dramatizes: the operations you did not run, which cost you a legend and bought you a year, and which you can never once point to as an achievement because their entire result is *the absence of a catastrophe.*

- @root: Continuar.

@fig_tomar
new-page: true
face-image: img/camioneta.jpg
on-arrival: {! Q.ch_p1="tomar"; Q.leyenda=(Q.leyenda||0)+4; Q.f_cerco+=25; if(Q.f_cerco>100){Q.f_cerco=100;} Q.coronel_activo=1; !}

= El hombre en el jacal

It takes ninety seconds on a curve above the río and nobody fires a shot. By nightfall the most powerful man in Guerrero is sitting on a stool in a hut in country the army will spend a fortune failing to find, being extremely polite to a nineteen-year-old with a rifle, and asking — repeatedly, with genuine bewilderment — what it is you actually want.

That is now the whole question. Whatever you ask for is what the movement will be understood to be, in every newspaper in Mexico, for the rest of its existence.

- @fig_d_dinero: Money. Enough to arm and feed the column for two years, and no speeches. *(the most useful; the word 'kidnappers' sticks permanently)*
- @fig_d_presos: The prisoners. Every campesino held without charge in the state, and the disappeared of the airbase accounted for. *(no money; the demand nobody can call banditry)*
- @fig_d_tierra: The land. The ejido titles his family has sat on since 1958, signed, filed, and read aloud on the radio. *(the demand that makes the state choose on camera)*

@fig_d_dinero
go-to: foco_eventos.fig_fin
on-arrival: {! Q.ch_p2="dinero"; !}

@fig_d_presos
go-to: foco_eventos.fig_fin
on-arrival: {! Q.ch_p2="presos"; !}

@fig_d_tierra
go-to: foco_eventos.fig_fin
on-arrival: {! Q.ch_p2="tierra"; !}

@fig_fin
new-page: true
face-image: img/Lucio.jpg
on-arrival: {!
var _p2=Q.ch_p2||"", _h=Math.max(0,(Q.apoyo_techo||55)-(Q.apoyo_popular||0));
if(_p2==="dinero"){
